Optimal Solution - Greedy solution using sorting and a stack to simulate car fleets merging by arrival time.
                   Process cars from closest to target; if a car behind arrives later, it forms a new fleet, otherwise it merges.

class Solution {
    public int carFleet(int target, int[] position, int[] speed) {
        // If only one car, it forms one fleet
        if (position.length == 1)  return 1;
        // Stack to store arrival times of fleets
        Stack<Double> st = new Stack<>();
        // Combine position and speed into a single 2D array
        int[][] combine = new int[position.length][2];
        for (int i = 0; i < position.length; i++) {
            combine[i][0] = position[i]; // position
            combine[i][1] = speed[i];    // speed
        }
        // Sort cars by position (ascending)
        Arrays.sort(combine, Comparator.comparingInt(o -> o[0]));
        // Traverse from the car closest to target
        for (int i = combine.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            // Time required to reach target
            double currTime = (double) (target - combine[i][0]) / combine[i][1];
            // If current car catches up to previous fleet
            if (!st.isEmpty() && currTime <= st.peek()) {
                continue;  // It joins the fleet
            }
            // Otherwise, it forms a new fleet
            st.push(currTime);
        }
        return st.size();
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(n log n). because of sorting the array.
Space Complexity :- O(n). because we are using stack.
